Ladybird shapes compatible text across unused soft-wrap opportunities,
and keeps the permitted break offsets separately. It reshapes the
pieces when a line needs a break. Min-content sizing measures each
piece independently, while line fitting includes the unbreakable
continuation in following inline boxes and fallback-font runs.

The shaping cache retains a conservative indication of contextual
substitution or positioning from HarfBuzz, including multi-unit
clusters. Runs without either keep their small independent chunks.
This avoids repeatedly shaping long suffixes of ordinary independent
text, and leaves the compact prepared-item representation unchanged.

Blink keeps a fitting shape in ShapingLineBreaker::ShapeLine, and
reshapes boundaries that need context when a line breaks. WebKit
measures candidate text ranges in TextUtil::width, and combines
compatible text items from one box in Line::appendText. Ladybird
matches their preservation of substitutions across unused breaks.

Gecko measures pre-shaped text with independent break flags in
gfxTextRun::BreakAndMeasureText. Its gfxTextRun::SetLineBreaks leaves
shaping unchanged at actual wraps. Ladybird instead reshapes the
chosen pieces: In the Chrome comparison, a wrapped programming arrow
uses the glyphs of each resulting line, not parts of the full arrow.

Chrome renders the Fira Code sequences identically with normal wrap,
word-break:break-all, and white-space:nowrap when they fit. Disabling
contextual substitutions removes the arrows. The narrow normal-wrap
cases match explicit line breaks, including an arrow in the suffix.
The intrinsic-width and adjacent-inline controls match Chrome too.

A separate punctuation-break limitation remains: word-break:break-all
omits some opportunities between consecutive hyphens that normal
wrapping permits. This change preserves those break rules; it fixes
shaping at the opportunities they already provide.
